WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:02.600
- Hey YouTubers, up next,

00:00:02.600 --> 00:00:06.083
StarTalk Cosmic Queries, Space Week.

00:00:06.083 --> 00:00:08.666
(upbeat music)

00:00:10.350 --> 00:00:12.510
This is StarTalk.

00:00:12.510 --> 00:00:13.900
I'm your host, Neil deGrasse Tyson,

00:00:13.900 --> 00:00:15.500
your personal astrophysicist.

00:00:15.500 --> 00:00:18.080
And this is a Cosmic Queries edition

00:00:18.080 --> 00:00:21.400
where we are celebrating Space Week.

00:00:21.400 --> 00:00:23.453
That would be the first week of October.

00:00:24.700 --> 00:00:27.000
That happens to contain my
birthday, I'm just sayin'.

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:28.770
- Woo, oh yeah.

00:00:28.770 --> 00:00:30.159
- Who's making those whooping noises?

00:00:30.159 --> 00:00:31.070
(laughing)

00:00:31.070 --> 00:00:32.890
I've got here my co-host
for this, Matt Kirshen.

00:00:32.890 --> 00:00:33.880
Matt, welcome back!

00:00:33.880 --> 00:00:34.750
- Thank you, Neil.

00:00:34.750 --> 00:00:35.880
- Excellent--

00:00:35.880 --> 00:00:39.090
- What came first by the way,
Space Week or your birthday?

00:00:39.090 --> 00:00:40.140
Did it become Space Week

00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:42.150
because it was the Neil
deGrasse Tyson birthday?

00:00:42.150 --> 00:00:45.010
- My birthday precedes
the official celebration

00:00:45.010 --> 00:00:45.843
of Space Week.

00:00:45.843 --> 00:00:47.120
- [Matt] There we go.
- So maybe!

00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:48.570
- Maybe that was the designation.

00:00:48.570 --> 00:00:49.883
- Maybe.

00:00:50.890 --> 00:00:53.370
We've got a lot of fun
questions coming up.

00:00:53.370 --> 00:00:55.900
- [Matt] We sure do.
- And I've got back up here.

00:00:55.900 --> 00:00:56.733
In fact, I'm his backup.

00:00:56.733 --> 00:00:59.270
If he's on the show
with me, I'm his backup.

00:00:59.270 --> 00:01:02.050
The one, the only, the
geek-in-chief, Charles Liu.

00:01:02.050 --> 00:01:03.606
- I am so happy to be here, Neil.

00:01:03.606 --> 00:01:04.439
Thank you.

00:01:04.439 --> 00:01:06.130
- Friend and colleague, you're a professor

00:01:06.130 --> 00:01:08.890
at City University of New
York at Staten Island.

00:01:08.890 --> 00:01:09.723
- Correct.

00:01:09.723 --> 00:01:10.830
- And you just had a book come out.

00:01:10.830 --> 00:01:12.080
- Yes, thank you.

00:01:12.080 --> 00:01:14.130
Yeah, it's called "30-Second Universe".

00:01:14.130 --> 00:01:16.146
It's published by Ivy Press in the UK.

00:01:16.146 --> 00:01:17.620
- Is it 'cause there
are 31 other universes

00:01:17.620 --> 00:01:19.266
that we're in the 32nd one?

00:01:19.266 --> 00:01:20.160
Is that what that means?

00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:21.830
- I asked the very same question.

00:01:21.830 --> 00:01:25.270
The parsing is 30 hypen Second space--

00:01:25.270 --> 00:01:27.976
- Every decision is made,
the Universe splits in two.

00:01:27.976 --> 00:01:29.220
(laughing)

00:01:29.220 --> 00:01:31.498
- He made 31 decisions that nobody wanted.

00:01:31.498 --> 00:01:36.150
- It's only 32 decisions,
that's only what, five?

00:01:36.150 --> 00:01:38.000
(laughing)
- Five doublings.

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:41.920
- That's right, no, yes,
it is about cosmology

00:01:41.920 --> 00:01:44.010
and I have two terrific co-authors,

00:01:44.010 --> 00:01:48.580
who are also astronomy and
physics professors and we talk--

00:01:48.580 --> 00:01:49.810
- [Neil] The authors are?

00:01:49.810 --> 00:01:53.660
- Karen Masters at Haverford College

00:01:53.660 --> 00:01:55.920
and Sevil Salur at Rutgers University.

00:01:55.920 --> 00:01:56.753
- [Neil] Okay, cool.

00:01:56.753 --> 00:01:58.040
- And we talk about cosmology,

00:01:58.040 --> 00:02:01.760
including the idea in quantum mechanics

00:02:01.760 --> 00:02:04.390
that you create many
worlds with a hypothesis--

00:02:04.390 --> 00:02:05.223
- With every split

00:02:05.223 --> 00:02:06.056
of a timeline, okay.
- [Charles] With every

00:02:06.056 --> 00:02:07.870
decision you make you
create a new timeline.

00:02:07.870 --> 00:02:11.810
- Which I believe was originally
formulated by the father

00:02:11.810 --> 00:02:14.362
of the lead singer of
one of my favorite bands.

00:02:14.362 --> 00:02:17.310
- Oohhh.

00:02:17.310 --> 00:02:19.170
- I believe it was Hugh Everett,

00:02:19.170 --> 00:02:20.483
the physicist who came up with that.

00:02:20.483 --> 00:02:22.870
- Hugh does get credit for that, yeah.

00:02:22.870 --> 00:02:25.150
- Whose son created the band, The Eagles.

00:02:25.150 --> 00:02:26.143
- No kidding!

00:02:26.980 --> 00:02:29.262
Another connection
between science and music.

00:02:29.262 --> 00:02:30.887
How 'bout that.
- You got it, you got it.

00:02:30.887 --> 00:02:33.880
- And Matt, you of course,
the host of Probably Science.

00:02:33.880 --> 00:02:34.713
- That's it!

00:02:34.713 --> 00:02:36.470
- And I said I would never
invite you back until it became

00:02:36.470 --> 00:02:37.780
Definitely Science.

00:02:37.780 --> 00:02:38.637
You're still not there.

00:02:38.637 --> 00:02:41.020
- The one episode that you
were kind enough to come on,

00:02:41.020 --> 00:02:42.141
that was the Definite episode.

00:02:42.141 --> 00:02:43.126
(laughing)

00:02:43.126 --> 00:02:43.959
And everything else,

00:02:43.959 --> 00:02:45.140
we're just failing.
- I forgot about that.

00:02:45.140 --> 00:02:46.280
- You hosted me on your show--

00:02:46.280 --> 00:02:48.160
- You were very kind
to come by that, yeah.

00:02:48.160 --> 00:02:50.020
- So, we've got questions
from our fan base

00:02:50.020 --> 00:02:52.740
as we usually solicit for Cosmic Queries

00:02:52.740 --> 00:02:55.240
and this is just, I haven't seen 'em,

00:02:55.240 --> 00:02:56.660
general questions about space.

00:02:56.660 --> 00:02:57.930
You're gonna hand pick 'em.

00:02:57.930 --> 00:02:58.763
- I am.

00:02:58.763 --> 00:03:00.550
- And you're gonna
throw down the gauntlet.

00:03:00.550 --> 00:03:01.646
- Ooh.

00:03:01.646 --> 00:03:04.572
- I'll sit here and
eat my lunch (laughing)

00:03:04.572 --> 00:03:05.900
while Charles answers all the que--

00:03:05.900 --> 00:03:07.550
That's why I bring him on, so
I don't have to work that way.

00:03:07.550 --> 00:03:08.590
- Okay, so I'll just--

00:03:08.590 --> 00:03:11.870
- We have very strongly
overlapping Venn diagrams

00:03:11.870 --> 00:03:13.210
in our expertise

00:03:13.210 --> 00:03:15.710
but he takes it another
dimension beyond that.

00:03:15.710 --> 00:03:16.873
- I'm sweatin' now, Neil,

00:03:16.873 --> 00:03:17.783
I don't know.
- So, we're good.

00:03:17.783 --> 00:03:18.616
- I don't know,
- We good.

00:03:18.616 --> 00:03:19.449
- we'll see how it goes.

00:03:19.449 --> 00:03:20.883
- I'm gonna start off with this one

00:03:20.883 --> 00:03:23.880
because this ties into what
we were just talking about.

00:03:23.880 --> 00:03:27.520
This is from DQFam, who says--

00:03:27.520 --> 00:03:28.353
- [Neil] What platform?

00:03:28.353 --> 00:03:31.386
- On Twitter, this is
DQ@flatknot on Twitter.

00:03:31.386 --> 00:03:32.480
(laughing)

00:03:32.480 --> 00:03:34.733
I think that's actually
relevant to this question

00:03:34.733 --> 00:03:38.017
because DQFam aka FlatKnot says,

00:03:38.017 --> 00:03:40.529
"Is there a parallel universe where I,

00:03:40.529 --> 00:03:43.460
as an astrophysicist on the flat earth,

00:03:43.460 --> 00:03:45.210
combat the disinformation agenda

00:03:45.210 --> 00:03:46.870
of the round earth society"?

00:03:46.870 --> 00:03:48.207
- Whoa!
- Ooooooh ooh!

00:03:50.220 --> 00:03:52.496
Way to start us off with an easy one.

00:03:52.496 --> 00:03:53.850
(laughing)

00:03:53.850 --> 00:03:55.902
Well, the short answer is no.

00:03:55.902 --> 00:03:57.429
'cause
- Next question. (laughing)

00:03:57.429 --> 00:03:58.480
- you're wrong.

00:03:58.480 --> 00:04:01.420
But I think we can take
this in a direction

00:04:01.420 --> 00:04:05.250
where we imagine that he
actually is in a flat universe,

00:04:05.250 --> 00:04:08.237
a two-dimensional universe
where somebody says,

00:04:08.237 --> 00:04:11.170
"No, actually the Universe
is three-dimensional".

00:04:11.170 --> 00:04:13.980
Now, remember the beautiful
book, "Flatland" from--

00:04:13.980 --> 00:04:15.240
- By Edwin Abbott, yep.

00:04:15.240 --> 00:04:16.750
- From decades ago.

00:04:16.750 --> 00:04:17.670
- A century ago, yeah.

00:04:17.670 --> 00:04:18.640
- It's been a century?

00:04:18.640 --> 00:04:19.623
- Yeah, it was a turn
- Oh my gosh, wow.

00:04:19.623 --> 00:04:20.623
- of the century book.

00:04:22.118 --> 00:04:23.500
Turn of the previous century.

00:04:23.500 --> 00:04:25.680
- Right, fair enough. (laughing)

00:04:25.680 --> 00:04:29.190
How can we prove that the
Universe is two-dimensional,

00:04:29.190 --> 00:04:31.840
if indeed it is two-dimensional,
where someone is claiming

00:04:31.840 --> 00:04:33.390
that there's a third dimension?

00:04:35.157 --> 00:04:36.890
I would approach it this way.

00:04:36.890 --> 00:04:38.253
- Well, you're talking
about the whole Universe.

00:04:38.253 --> 00:04:39.890
The dude's just talking about Earth.

00:04:39.890 --> 00:04:40.723
- Right.

00:04:41.560 --> 00:04:42.960
- I guess if the Universe
is two dimensions,

00:04:42.960 --> 00:04:44.250
the Earth has to be a flat disc.

00:04:44.250 --> 00:04:45.083
- Correct.

00:04:45.083 --> 00:04:45.916
- Oh, gotcha, fine, fine.
- Right.

00:04:45.916 --> 00:04:50.880
So in that sense, then some
non-scientific individual

00:04:50.880 --> 00:04:51.713
comes along and says,

00:04:51.713 --> 00:04:54.070
"The Universe is a
three-dimensional structure

00:04:54.070 --> 00:04:55.970
and the Earth is actually a
three-dimensional structure".

00:04:55.970 --> 00:04:57.343
How would you counter that?

00:04:58.520 --> 00:05:01.520
And so that makes it a
more interesting question.

00:05:01.520 --> 00:05:04.410
I would say one of the first
things you would try to do,

00:05:04.410 --> 00:05:09.110
is to see if you can prove that
the two-dimensional Universe

00:05:09.110 --> 00:05:11.560
cannot project into the third.

00:05:11.560 --> 00:05:16.560
In other words, you cannot find
a way to extend the Universe

00:05:17.380 --> 00:05:19.313
in an orthogonal direction to both--

00:05:19.313 --> 00:05:20.430
- Perpendicular.

00:05:20.430 --> 00:05:23.210
- Excuse me, yeah, you can't
extend something perpendicular

00:05:23.210 --> 00:05:26.480
to both length and width.

00:05:26.480 --> 00:05:29.550
Now, can you do that in any--

00:05:29.550 --> 00:05:31.470
- What you're saying is
in our current Universe,

00:05:31.470 --> 00:05:32.303
- [Charles] Yeah.

00:05:32.303 --> 00:05:33.840
- we have a height, width, depth

00:05:33.840 --> 00:05:34.673
- [Charles] Yes.

00:05:34.673 --> 00:05:38.240
- and you're saying, the
version of that question to us

00:05:38.240 --> 00:05:42.160
would be, find another
place to stick out a line

00:05:42.160 --> 00:05:44.290
that's at right angles to
those three other right angles.

00:05:44.290 --> 00:05:45.123
- [Charles] Yes.
- And we don't know how

00:05:45.123 --> 00:05:46.070
to do that.
- Right.

00:05:46.070 --> 00:05:47.940
And so that would be way that
- How do you do that?

00:05:47.940 --> 00:05:50.431
- someone in a true
two-dimensional universe

00:05:50.431 --> 00:05:53.182
is trying to say that there
is no third dimension.

00:05:53.182 --> 00:05:55.790
- Am I right in thinking
that there are some theories

00:05:55.790 --> 00:05:59.250
of physics that involve
highev spatial dimensions

00:05:59.250 --> 00:06:00.083
in our universe?

00:06:00.083 --> 00:06:04.370
- Oh, completely, but those
dimensions are not visible to us

00:06:04.370 --> 00:06:05.203
- Okay.

00:06:05.203 --> 00:06:07.840
- And one of the well
known formulations of this,

00:06:07.840 --> 00:06:09.763
is something called a
Compactified dimension.

00:06:09.763 --> 00:06:11.300
- But Charles, you're okay with this.

00:06:11.300 --> 00:06:13.320
There are these extra dimensions,
you just can't see them!

00:06:13.320 --> 00:06:14.153
You're okay with this?

00:06:14.153 --> 00:06:15.850
- I am not okay with it, yet.

00:06:15.850 --> 00:06:16.683
- Okay. (laughing)

00:06:16.683 --> 00:06:18.240
- But they haven't been disproven.

00:06:18.240 --> 00:06:19.073
- Okay.

00:06:19.073 --> 00:06:21.400
- Here's the idea of the
Compactified dimension.

00:06:21.400 --> 00:06:24.260
If our length was actually,

00:06:24.260 --> 00:06:26.090
you zoom in further
and further and further

00:06:26.090 --> 00:06:27.430
and you find out that our length is

00:06:27.430 --> 00:06:30.000
actually has length, width and height

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:33.120
but it's so small compared to even atoms

00:06:33.120 --> 00:06:36.800
or sub-atomic particles
that that width and height

00:06:36.800 --> 00:06:38.920
is not detectable in our Universe.

00:06:38.920 --> 00:06:40.013
- It's tantamount to just
being a one-dimensional--

00:06:40.013 --> 00:06:41.760
- It's as if it were one-dimensional

00:06:41.760 --> 00:06:43.270
for all intensive purposes--

00:06:43.270 --> 00:06:45.394
- Is that where they use
the word, compactified?

00:06:45.394 --> 00:06:47.860
It's contained tightly within

00:06:47.860 --> 00:06:49.430
the other dimensions
- Right, you can imagine

00:06:49.430 --> 00:06:52.000
for example, looking
at the Alaska Pipeline.

00:06:52.000 --> 00:06:54.620
If you're standing right
next to the Alaska Pipeline,

00:06:54.620 --> 00:06:59.170
it is obviously both with
length and width, additionally

00:06:59.170 --> 00:07:00.930
it has height, so it's a
three-dimensional object.

00:07:00.930 --> 00:07:01.940
You step a little further away,

00:07:01.940 --> 00:07:03.630
it just becomes like a long strip,

00:07:03.630 --> 00:07:05.670
so you think it has height and length

00:07:05.670 --> 00:07:07.040
but you can't see the depth anymore.

00:07:07.040 --> 00:07:10.210
And then if you fly away, say
to Canada and you're looking

00:07:10.210 --> 00:07:11.820
at the Alaska Pipeline from a distance,

00:07:11.820 --> 00:07:13.450
it only looks like a line.

00:07:13.450 --> 00:07:14.510
By that same token,

00:07:14.510 --> 00:07:18.300
we could compactify dimensions
into our linear dimension.

00:07:18.300 --> 00:07:19.890
- So, that's how you get
the higher dimensions

00:07:19.890 --> 00:07:21.140
even though you can't experience them.

00:07:21.140 --> 00:07:21.973
- Right.
- Okay.

00:07:21.973 --> 00:07:23.280
- And somehow, if you think

00:07:23.280 --> 00:07:27.180
that those Compactified
dimensions can affect our Universe

00:07:27.180 --> 00:07:30.730
in ways we can't measure,
then you can say,

00:07:30.730 --> 00:07:32.070
set up a universe with
multiple dimensions.

00:07:32.070 --> 00:07:33.910
- And what you're saying
is, we're too big.

00:07:33.910 --> 00:07:34.743
- We are.

00:07:36.063 --> 00:07:36.896
Isn't that cool?

00:07:36.896 --> 00:07:37.729
- Yeah, that's cool.

00:07:37.729 --> 00:07:39.740
- Yeah, and as a universe for
which we are very, very tiny,

00:07:39.740 --> 00:07:41.660
we are still way too big to see

00:07:41.660 --> 00:07:43.440
hypothesized compactified dimensions.

00:07:43.440 --> 00:07:47.790
- And we're too small to
see that Earth is a sphere.

00:07:47.790 --> 00:07:49.589
- Yes, yes.

00:07:49.589 --> 00:07:51.615
- We just walk in a straight line

00:07:51.615 --> 00:07:54.010
and it's a straight line to you

00:07:54.010 --> 00:07:55.440
but if you back out, there's,

00:07:55.440 --> 00:07:57.140
you're walking on the surface of a sphere

00:07:57.140 --> 00:07:58.437
and you don't even know it.
- Fantastic, that's right

00:07:58.437 --> 00:08:01.610
and so that's how you try
to address, two-dimension,

00:08:01.610 --> 00:08:03.260
three-dimension, more-dimension type--

00:08:03.260 --> 00:08:04.356
- I love the question.

00:08:04.356 --> 00:08:05.270
Really good question.
- Yeah, very clever.

00:08:05.270 --> 00:08:07.960
- I love the question
and I love that answer.

00:08:07.960 --> 00:08:08.793
This is more of a practical--

00:08:08.793 --> 00:08:10.714
- That's why we have him on the show!

00:08:10.714 --> 00:08:14.091
It's not an accident, he
didn't just drop off the cart,

00:08:14.091 --> 00:08:15.660
the vegetable cart.
- I don't know.

00:08:15.660 --> 00:08:17.124
I thought it was for
the snacks that I bring.

00:08:17.124 --> 00:08:18.220
(laughing)

00:08:18.220 --> 00:08:20.490
- They were great snacks.
- Thank you.

00:08:20.490 --> 00:08:24.608
- So, this is more of a
practical question from

00:08:24.608 --> 00:08:27.297
Seamus Star on Instagram.

00:08:27.297 --> 00:08:30.117
"Will we focus more on habitable planets

00:08:30.117 --> 00:08:31.987
"or ore rich planets in the future"?

00:08:31.987 --> 00:08:34.925
That's ore, O-R-E, rich planets.

00:08:34.925 --> 00:08:36.531
- Ore rich planets.

00:08:36.531 --> 00:08:39.580
- Ore, ore rich planets (laughing)

00:08:39.580 --> 00:08:41.600
- 'Cause I would focus on rich planets,

00:08:41.600 --> 00:08:42.727
if it's just "or rich"

00:08:42.727 --> 00:08:43.560
(laughing)

00:08:43.560 --> 00:08:45.750
'cause then I could do business with them.

00:08:45.750 --> 00:08:49.550
But ore rich planets, I
think are not as interesting

00:08:49.550 --> 00:08:50.610
as habitable planets

00:08:50.610 --> 00:08:54.660
and the reason is to go to another planet

00:08:54.660 --> 00:08:59.130
that is trillions of miles
away, to get a little bit of ore

00:08:59.130 --> 00:09:03.090
and then bring it back to
Earth is really impractical.

00:09:03.090 --> 00:09:06.060
You can't hardly imagine a substance

00:09:06.060 --> 00:09:08.700
currently known to science for which

00:09:08.700 --> 00:09:12.030
that would be profitable
or economically feasible.

00:09:12.030 --> 00:09:14.550
You have, of course, the
science fiction world

00:09:14.550 --> 00:09:16.500
where you imagine something called,

00:09:16.500 --> 00:09:18.080
oh I don't know, Unobtainium.

00:09:18.080 --> 00:09:18.913
- Sure, yeah.

00:09:18.913 --> 00:09:20.790
- Which could not be
obtained here on Earth

00:09:20.790 --> 00:09:23.320
and therefore you must
go far away to get it

00:09:23.320 --> 00:09:26.740
but that seems extremely unlikely to me.

00:09:26.740 --> 00:09:30.520
So, I would much rather find
the blue-skinned creatures

00:09:30.520 --> 00:09:31.803
than the Unobtainium.

00:09:32.750 --> 00:09:37.500
- I agree with you scientifically
but as a practical guy,

00:09:37.500 --> 00:09:42.500
as a realist, I think the
drive to obtain resources

00:09:44.410 --> 00:09:47.030
and possibly Unobtainium would
be greater than the drive

00:09:47.030 --> 00:09:47.910
to find other life.

00:09:47.910 --> 00:09:49.580
- [Charles] Oh.
- And!

00:09:49.580 --> 00:09:52.840
the more busy we are in space,
the less will be the need

00:09:52.840 --> 00:09:54.811
to bring the resources back to Earth,

00:09:54.811 --> 00:09:56.510
compared with bringing the resources

00:09:56.510 --> 00:09:58.030
to other on-site locations.

00:09:58.030 --> 00:09:58.863
- Oh!

00:09:58.863 --> 00:10:00.010
- So that has value.

00:10:00.010 --> 00:10:02.070
- You're thinking much further
in the future than I am.

00:10:02.070 --> 00:10:02.903
- Yeah.

00:10:02.903 --> 00:10:04.860
- Yeah, that makes a lot of
sense 'cause I can certainly see

00:10:04.860 --> 00:10:07.280
- And then (mumbles),
asteroids can have this.

00:10:07.280 --> 00:10:09.960
- Now, that's the thing
that I wanted to point out.

00:10:09.960 --> 00:10:12.010
- I'd still claim the first trillionaire,

00:10:13.479 --> 00:10:14.874
will be the person who exploits

00:10:14.874 --> 00:10:16.847
the natural resources of asteroids.

00:10:16.847 --> 00:10:19.230
- Well, there is a
connected question here from

00:10:19.230 --> 00:10:21.370
SciGuyKai on Twitter.

00:10:21.370 --> 00:10:22.357
- Okay.

00:10:22.357 --> 00:10:26.757
- "What are the most
common precious metals

00:10:26.757 --> 00:10:29.827
"and useful elements found in asteroids?

00:10:29.827 --> 00:10:32.147
"Could we mine asteroids
to build components

00:10:32.147 --> 00:10:34.757
"for star ships in space shipyards?"

00:10:35.680 --> 00:10:38.490
- I would say the rare
earths are more important

00:10:38.490 --> 00:10:39.608
for asteroid mining.

00:10:39.608 --> 00:10:41.783
- Yeah, I think all of 'em.

00:10:41.783 --> 00:10:43.300
- Microchips and things like that.

00:10:43.300 --> 00:10:44.320
- Yeah, the rare earths,

00:10:44.320 --> 00:10:46.090
by the way the rare earths
are not particularly rare,

00:10:46.090 --> 00:10:47.657
they're just mostly in China.

00:10:47.657 --> 00:10:49.220
(laughing)

00:10:49.220 --> 00:10:53.787
So, access to them is
not as free and smooth

00:10:53.787 --> 00:10:55.597
as we might want.
- Right.

00:10:55.597 --> 00:10:58.270
- For the future of our technology.

00:10:58.270 --> 00:10:59.969
And as Charles noted,

00:10:59.969 --> 00:11:02.123
the rare earth elements are fundamental to

00:11:02.123 --> 00:11:04.760
what is our electronic components

00:11:04.760 --> 00:11:06.710
to making them work and do their thing.

00:11:09.720 --> 00:11:12.900
I think it's the first
trillionaire and they're all there

00:11:12.900 --> 00:11:13.733
on the asteroid.

00:11:13.733 --> 00:11:14.950
The asteroid's got 'em
- They are there.

00:11:14.950 --> 00:11:18.430
The challenge is to
get them back to Earth.

00:11:18.430 --> 00:11:20.960
You gotta get there, fine but
then you have to mine them

00:11:20.960 --> 00:11:21.870
and you gotta bring them back

00:11:21.870 --> 00:11:24.660
and mining is actually pretty
complicated, there's a lot of

00:11:24.660 --> 00:11:26.450
- Build the ship in space, dude.

00:11:26.450 --> 00:11:27.870
Don't bring it back to Earth.

00:11:27.870 --> 00:11:30.093
It's a waste of a whole journey.

00:11:30.093 --> 00:11:32.203
That's what the question asked, right?

00:11:32.203 --> 00:11:33.980
- Then how do you get the people

00:11:33.980 --> 00:11:35.810
from Earth to the spaceship?

00:11:35.810 --> 00:11:36.930
You still need another spaceship.

00:11:36.930 --> 00:11:39.410
- Robots do it, no,
robots build the spaceship

00:11:39.410 --> 00:11:42.230
and then you go up when
it's time. (laughing)

00:11:42.230 --> 00:11:43.660
- Why not, sure?

00:11:43.660 --> 00:11:45.140
- There's an app for that.

00:11:45.140 --> 00:11:46.760
Your spaceship is ready, sir.

00:11:46.760 --> 00:11:49.050
- You are definitely looking much further

00:11:49.050 --> 00:11:50.800
along the time horizon than I am.

00:11:50.800 --> 00:11:51.693
- Okay.

00:11:51.693 --> 00:11:52.526
- And I think that's a fair statement.

00:11:52.526 --> 00:11:55.860
I will agree with you if we're
talking centuries not decades

00:11:55.860 --> 00:11:59.210
- Money drives things more
than curiosity unfortunately.

00:11:59.210 --> 00:12:02.260
The ideal part is to combine
the curiosity with the money.

00:12:02.260 --> 00:12:03.093
- Yeah.

00:12:03.093 --> 00:12:04.643
- And then it happens like that, yeah.

00:12:05.760 --> 00:12:10.272
- Here is a Twitter question
from George Zenofuntus

00:12:10.272 --> 00:12:11.507
This is more of an Earth-based question.

00:12:11.507 --> 00:12:14.577
"Why haven't there been a
greater number of advancements

00:12:14.577 --> 00:12:17.300
"on solar energy, given
that's it's literally

00:12:17.300 --> 00:12:19.480
a constant free source of energy"?

00:12:19.480 --> 00:12:20.556
- Okay.
- Yeah, Charles.

00:12:20.556 --> 00:12:23.030
- I have been on my soapbox on this

00:12:23.030 --> 00:12:26.403
with my astronomy students
ever since I started teaching.

00:12:27.460 --> 00:12:31.610
Solar energy is so
plentiful and it's so free.

00:12:31.610 --> 00:12:34.130
You make the calculation, in fact--

00:12:34.130 --> 00:12:35.050
- What does "so free" mean?

00:12:35.050 --> 00:12:36.327
- Okay, that's my point.

00:12:36.327 --> 00:12:38.400
That's my point.
- Free is free.

00:12:38.400 --> 00:12:42.630
- It means that I don't
need to pay for the energy

00:12:42.630 --> 00:12:46.960
to arrive to my house, that's the free.

00:12:46.960 --> 00:12:50.510
The rest of it is the
thing that costs money.

00:12:50.510 --> 00:12:55.510
Think about this, the amount
of energy that the sun sends

00:12:55.670 --> 00:12:57.550
onto the surface of the Earth

00:12:57.550 --> 00:13:02.550
is what we call a unit of
insolation, insolation with an 'o'

00:13:02.770 --> 00:13:03.890
as opposed to insulation.

00:13:03.890 --> 00:13:04.723
- That's a real word?

00:13:04.723 --> 00:13:05.556
- Yeah, insolation
- I didn't know that.

00:13:05.556 --> 00:13:06.610
- The earth scientists use it.

00:13:06.610 --> 00:13:08.176
That's why we don't know it really.

00:13:08.176 --> 00:13:10.580
- Insolation.
- Insolation, yeah.

00:13:10.580 --> 00:13:13.150
- Is just amount of sunlight?

00:13:13.150 --> 00:13:15.310
- Sol is latin for sun.

00:13:15.310 --> 00:13:17.093
- It's the amount of
sunlight that hits the Earth,

00:13:17.093 --> 00:13:21.500
well any planet, but in this
case, the insolation onto Earth

00:13:21.500 --> 00:13:26.500
is at a rate of 1400
watts per square meter.

00:13:27.849 --> 00:13:29.427
What does that mean?

00:13:29.427 --> 00:13:33.200
Our typical led light
bulb is now 14 watts.

00:13:33.200 --> 00:13:34.033
- At most!

00:13:34.033 --> 00:13:38.550
- Yeah, right, so on, just
on the table in front of us,

00:13:38.550 --> 00:13:40.060
just on any--

00:13:40.060 --> 00:13:40.893
- [Neil] Coffee table

00:13:40.893 --> 00:13:43.970
- Yeah, coffee table, on any surface

00:13:43.970 --> 00:13:47.170
you are getting enough light energy

00:13:47.170 --> 00:13:49.430
if it were properly converted

00:13:49.430 --> 00:13:52.010
to be able to illuminate

00:13:52.010 --> 00:13:53.983
a hundred light bulbs worth of light.

00:13:55.960 --> 00:13:56.793
- Continuously.

00:13:56.793 --> 00:13:57.995
- [Charles] Continuously forever.

00:13:57.995 --> 00:13:58.874
- A hundred light bulbs.

00:13:58.874 --> 00:13:59.707
- [Charles] A hundred light bulbs.

00:13:59.707 --> 00:14:00.540
- Most houses don't have
a hundred light bulbs.

00:14:00.540 --> 00:14:03.840
- That's right, so you can
literally light every house

00:14:03.840 --> 00:14:06.610
in the world on this free energy,

00:14:06.610 --> 00:14:09.640
if we just figured out a way to exploit it

00:14:09.640 --> 00:14:13.020
and I believe, sadly, that the main reason

00:14:13.020 --> 00:14:14.740
we have not done so is

00:14:14.740 --> 00:14:17.330
because people find it more profitable

00:14:17.330 --> 00:14:20.150
to exploit energy sources

00:14:20.150 --> 00:14:23.460
that are much older in their origins.

00:14:23.460 --> 00:14:24.293
Our ability--

00:14:24.293 --> 00:14:28.929
- Just say it, oil, just
say it, say it, oil!

00:14:28.929 --> 00:14:31.262
- Oooooother energy sources.

00:14:33.427 --> 00:14:38.427
I don't want to dis
older sources of energy

00:14:38.600 --> 00:14:41.708
because as much trouble
as they have caused us

00:14:41.708 --> 00:14:44.420
on environmental levels, they have also

00:14:44.420 --> 00:14:45.880
advanced civilization
- [Neil] And war.

00:14:45.880 --> 00:14:49.010
- Yeah, they've advanced
us so much in so many ways

00:14:49.010 --> 00:14:50.990
in the past couple of centuries.

00:14:50.990 --> 00:14:51.823
Gol, coil.

00:14:51.823 --> 00:14:55.365
- Gol, coil. (laughing)

00:14:55.365 --> 00:14:57.490
- Oil, coal

00:14:57.490 --> 00:15:01.100
natural gas, these fossil
fuels have really spurred

00:15:01.100 --> 00:15:03.870
our human ability to
be where we are today.

00:15:03.870 --> 00:15:06.170
But it is time to switch over

00:15:06.170 --> 00:15:08.790
and as with anything in society,

00:15:08.790 --> 00:15:11.320
switching over from something
old to something new

00:15:11.320 --> 00:15:13.570
takes time, there's resistance.

00:15:13.570 --> 00:15:16.030
The resistance should
move away at this point.

00:15:16.030 --> 00:15:19.210
There is no more reason
for us not to switch,

00:15:19.210 --> 00:15:22.770
except for trying to
preserve the prosperity

00:15:22.770 --> 00:15:25.700
in the interests of those
who have become wealthy

00:15:25.700 --> 00:15:26.880
from the old technology.

00:15:26.880 --> 00:15:29.720
- I've seen technologies, I
don't know how far along it is

00:15:29.720 --> 00:15:32.820
but I know they've trialed
things like road surfaces

00:15:32.820 --> 00:15:35.530
that have solar cells built into them.

00:15:35.530 --> 00:15:39.180
- That's right, you could
put solar cells on buildings,

00:15:39.180 --> 00:15:42.310
not just on the roofs
but there are windows now

00:15:42.310 --> 00:15:44.680
where people can put a film on them

00:15:44.680 --> 00:15:47.090
and if you want shade in your room,

00:15:47.090 --> 00:15:50.760
you just turn the film over
and then all that sunlight

00:15:50.760 --> 00:15:51.880
instead of going into your room

00:15:51.880 --> 00:15:53.570
gets absorbed by this film

00:15:53.570 --> 00:15:56.120
which can then be
converted into electricity.

00:15:56.120 --> 00:15:58.170
As long as we are willing

00:15:58.170 --> 00:16:01.436
to take a short-term hit on profits,

00:16:01.436 --> 00:16:06.436
we will have a long-term benefit
by using more solar energy.

00:16:06.710 --> 00:16:08.720
- Maybe it won't happen until

00:16:08.720 --> 00:16:11.490
we figure out how to make
it instantly profitable.

00:16:11.490 --> 00:16:16.490
Who would've thought that after
millennia of riding horses

00:16:17.096 --> 00:16:21.345
within a dozen, 15 years,
we switched over to cars?

00:16:21.345 --> 00:16:22.178
- [Charles] It was amazing.

00:16:22.178 --> 00:16:23.530
- At some point, somebody said,

00:16:23.530 --> 00:16:25.267
this car is more economical than my horse,

00:16:25.267 --> 00:16:26.100
- [Matt] Right.
- [Charles] Right.

00:16:26.100 --> 00:16:27.150
- than feeding my horse

00:16:27.150 --> 00:16:29.300
and the care and feeding of a huge animal.

00:16:29.300 --> 00:16:31.200
And so, at some point that happened,

00:16:31.200 --> 00:16:33.470
'cause nobody had to have special programs

00:16:33.470 --> 00:16:34.900
to get you to buy a car.

00:16:34.900 --> 00:16:37.150
All right, everybody bought cars.

00:16:37.150 --> 00:16:40.561
- Is there a tipping point?
- That's the point.

00:16:40.561 --> 00:16:43.150
- I don't see an obvious one.
- Neither do I.

00:16:43.150 --> 00:16:44.080
- Unfortunately.

00:16:44.080 --> 00:16:47.273
- How does it work, also the resources,

00:16:47.273 --> 00:16:48.106
what you were just talking about,

00:16:48.106 --> 00:16:51.393
mining for rare metals and
elements in the Universe.

00:16:52.520 --> 00:16:55.170
The resources needed
currently to make solar cells,

00:16:55.170 --> 00:16:56.670
do we have enough of those?

00:16:56.670 --> 00:16:57.503
- Yes.

00:16:57.503 --> 00:16:58.336
- [Neil] I don't know,
Charles, do we, yeah we do.

00:16:58.336 --> 00:17:00.350
- The vast majority of what we need

00:17:00.350 --> 00:17:02.730
is just really good silicon

00:17:02.730 --> 00:17:03.563
- Okay.

00:17:03.563 --> 00:17:05.190
- and so that's sand.

00:17:05.190 --> 00:17:07.910
We can basically take sand
from pretty much anywhere

00:17:07.910 --> 00:17:08.960
and turn them into solar cells.

00:17:08.960 --> 00:17:11.030
- [Neil] Silicon dioxide, SiO2

00:17:11.030 --> 00:17:15.300
- It is not hard, it's just
at the moment it costs money

00:17:15.300 --> 00:17:17.550
and people would rather
not spend that money.

00:17:18.730 --> 00:17:20.687
I myself am guilty of saying,

00:17:20.687 --> 00:17:23.626
"Gee, I should really put
solar panels on my roof."

00:17:23.626 --> 00:17:24.459
- And then you just go home

00:17:24.459 --> 00:17:25.970
and eat a cheeseburger and watch TV.

00:17:25.970 --> 00:17:29.550
- Yes, because it is much
less expensive for me

00:17:29.550 --> 00:17:32.930
as a consumer to heat my home
with natural gas right now

00:17:32.930 --> 00:17:35.120
or to light my home with coal power.

00:17:35.120 --> 00:17:36.900
- First adopters tend to be rich

00:17:36.900 --> 00:17:38.760
and they'll do it so
they have bragging rights

00:17:38.760 --> 00:17:41.490
that they're more green than you are.

00:17:41.490 --> 00:17:44.970
That helps change the
infrastructure when they have it.

00:17:44.970 --> 00:17:47.860
- The good news is that prices

00:17:47.860 --> 00:17:50.588
for solar electricity generating things

00:17:50.588 --> 00:17:52.690
are going down rapidly.

00:17:52.690 --> 00:17:54.810
- I will add before we take a quick break,

00:17:54.810 --> 00:17:59.767
that solar power is also hydroelectric

00:18:01.310 --> 00:18:03.340
because how do you think the
water gets up to the lake

00:18:03.340 --> 00:18:04.981
before it comes through the turbine?

00:18:04.981 --> 00:18:05.814
(laughing)

00:18:05.814 --> 00:18:06.647
- Evaporation.
- The sun evaporates it

00:18:06.647 --> 00:18:07.480
off the ocean
- Rain.

00:18:07.480 --> 00:18:09.840
- becomes a cloud, the cloud
moves over land, it rains.

00:18:09.840 --> 00:18:13.470
That's technically solar
power and so is wind energy,

00:18:13.470 --> 00:18:14.620
it's all from the sun.

00:18:14.620 --> 00:18:16.180
The unequal heating of the Earth's surface

00:18:16.180 --> 00:18:18.690
creates air currents that drive,

00:18:18.690 --> 00:18:20.460
so the sun manifests in more

00:18:20.460 --> 00:18:22.140
than just whether you need a solar panel.

00:18:22.140 --> 00:18:23.750
We gotta take a quick break
and when we come back,

00:18:23.750 --> 00:18:27.263
more of our Cosmic Queries,
Space Week edition.

00:18:29.020 --> 00:18:32.980
We're back on StarTalk Cosmic
Queries, Space Week edition.

00:18:32.980 --> 00:18:34.770
- [Neil] Charles Liu,
friend and colleague.

00:18:34.770 --> 00:18:35.603
- Thank you.

00:18:35.603 --> 00:18:36.690
- He's my expertise here

00:18:36.690 --> 00:18:38.640
and I'm just gonna color comment when,

00:18:38.640 --> 00:18:40.862
if I think he misses something,
which he hardly ever does,

00:18:40.862 --> 00:18:44.542
I'm in there, but generally
I'm not saying a thing.

00:18:44.542 --> 00:18:45.375
(laughing)

00:18:45.375 --> 00:18:47.160
Matt Kirshen welcome back.

00:18:47.160 --> 00:18:47.993
- Thank you so much.

00:18:47.993 --> 00:18:49.891
- [Neil] All right, take us
there, you got questions.

00:18:49.891 --> 00:18:50.724
- All right, I do.

00:18:50.724 --> 00:18:52.880
- Cull from our fan base,
I love this fan base.

00:18:52.880 --> 00:18:54.400
- [Matt] This one is from--

00:18:54.400 --> 00:18:56.230
- They're snarky and
they're funny and they're--

00:18:56.230 --> 00:18:57.083
- Smart.
- Yeah, and smart.

00:18:57.083 --> 00:18:58.520
- Really, really good questions.

00:18:58.520 --> 00:18:59.930
- This one's from Kyle Yocum,

00:18:59.930 --> 00:19:01.837
who has helpfully given
a pronunciation guide.

00:19:01.837 --> 00:19:03.690
(laughing)

00:19:03.690 --> 00:19:04.740
- That's in case we have Chuck Nice

00:19:04.740 --> 00:19:06.479
'cause Chuck can't pronounce anything.

00:19:06.479 --> 00:19:07.312
(laughing)

00:19:07.312 --> 00:19:09.847
- Kyle is from Tennessee and asks,

00:19:09.847 --> 00:19:11.650
"How would we identify something

00:19:11.650 --> 00:19:14.330
as truly being of another universe,

00:19:14.330 --> 00:19:17.280
rather than just a newly
discovered part of our own?"

00:19:17.280 --> 00:19:18.113
- Oh wow!

00:19:18.113 --> 00:19:20.621
- And I think I know the answer
to that, and it's antennae.

00:19:20.621 --> 00:19:22.770
(laughing)

00:19:22.770 --> 00:19:25.540
- Yeah, just tell the Andorians that,

00:19:25.540 --> 00:19:27.380
they'd be very irritated.

00:19:27.380 --> 00:19:29.080
No, they're from our universe too.

00:19:30.141 --> 00:19:30.974
- That's a good one, Charles.

00:19:30.974 --> 00:19:34.310
- I would have to think to chemistry.

00:19:34.310 --> 00:19:39.160
If an object is made of
something that is an element

00:19:39.160 --> 00:19:41.400
of something we have never seen before.

00:19:41.400 --> 00:19:43.740
Something that wouldn't
fit on our periodic table

00:19:43.740 --> 00:19:44.970
for any reason.

00:19:44.970 --> 00:19:47.100
That one would have to come from a place

00:19:47.100 --> 00:19:49.000
where the laws of physics are different

00:19:49.000 --> 00:19:50.360
and thus a different universe.

00:19:50.360 --> 00:19:52.460
- I agree entirely.

00:19:52.460 --> 00:19:55.030
Our periodic table of elements is full.

00:19:55.030 --> 00:19:58.340
We're incrementing at
the top end in our labs

00:19:58.340 --> 00:20:02.220
but there are no more slots at all, okay.

00:20:02.220 --> 00:20:05.280
And if they come up with
something that is a base element

00:20:05.280 --> 00:20:08.550
then it's gonna be an element
configured in some way

00:20:08.550 --> 00:20:10.400
that our elements are not configured.

00:20:10.400 --> 00:20:14.750
And we say, "Yep, this is
a weird bird right here".

00:20:14.750 --> 00:20:17.540
- It's similar in a sense
that an expanded version

00:20:17.540 --> 00:20:21.170
of how we know that there is
material in our Solar System

00:20:21.170 --> 00:20:22.940
that came from outside our Solar System

00:20:22.940 --> 00:20:24.740
before the sun was born.

00:20:24.740 --> 00:20:29.740
There are grains that contain
carbon nanodiamonds basically.

00:20:29.855 --> 00:20:31.627
- It doesn't have to be
before our sun was born.

00:20:31.627 --> 00:20:32.877
It doesn't have to be.

00:20:32.877 --> 00:20:34.210
- [Charles] Well, that's true.
- It could just be visiting.

00:20:34.210 --> 00:20:35.280
- Yeah, it could just be visiting,

00:20:35.280 --> 00:20:37.340
like floating into our Solar System.

00:20:37.340 --> 00:20:39.550
But we know if it's from
outside our Solar System

00:20:39.550 --> 00:20:43.970
if we compare their isotopic
ratios or their structures

00:20:43.970 --> 00:20:48.040
and we see that there's
nothing that matches that age

00:20:48.040 --> 00:20:50.110
or that composition
- [Neil] Mixture, yeah.

00:20:50.110 --> 00:20:52.660
- that is actually of our Solar System.

00:20:52.660 --> 00:20:54.400
- We have ways to do this.

00:20:54.400 --> 00:20:57.182
That's fine but then you
say, "How did it get here?"

00:20:57.182 --> 00:20:58.920
(laughing)

00:20:58.920 --> 00:21:01.120
What portal through the
space-time continuum

00:21:01.120 --> 00:21:02.650
connected two different universes

00:21:02.650 --> 00:21:03.995
with two different laws of physics?

00:21:03.995 --> 00:21:06.650
That's what the real issue will be.

00:21:06.650 --> 00:21:07.870
- Well, I was going to
do a different question,

00:21:07.870 --> 00:21:09.440
but since you talked about portals

00:21:09.440 --> 00:21:11.550
through the space-time
continuum, I'm going--

00:21:11.550 --> 00:21:12.650
- You went there, Neil.

00:21:12.650 --> 00:21:13.889
- I went there.
- [Charles] You went there.

00:21:13.889 --> 00:21:16.051
- I went there first.

00:21:16.051 --> 00:21:21.007
- I'm gonna go with Tony in
the 845 on Twitter who asked,

00:21:22.087 --> 00:21:24.857
"Even if we somehow
discover the tech to travel

00:21:24.857 --> 00:21:26.687
"at or faster than light,

00:21:26.687 --> 00:21:30.060
"would the passengers
of the vehicle survive?"

00:21:30.060 --> 00:21:33.573
- Oh, well, you know how
the Flash does it right?

00:21:34.913 --> 00:21:36.600
- Does he have atmospheric
separators in front of him?

00:21:36.600 --> 00:21:40.400
- Yes, the Flash can go
up to the speed of light,

00:21:40.400 --> 00:21:42.800
superhero fake science, all that right?

00:21:42.800 --> 00:21:44.360
- But if he's giving someone a piggyback?

00:21:44.360 --> 00:21:46.067
- Yeah, that person is toast.

00:21:46.067 --> 00:21:46.917
- Really!?

00:21:48.287 --> 00:21:52.660
Note to self, when offered
a piggyback ride by Flash.

00:21:52.660 --> 00:21:53.819
- What are we doing?

00:21:53.819 --> 00:21:55.530
Are we just doing a donkey
ride around the living room

00:21:55.530 --> 00:21:56.750
or are we going at Flash speed?

00:21:56.750 --> 00:21:58.320
- I'm taking the bus on that one.

00:21:58.320 --> 00:22:02.215
- Well, yeah, so the issue here is that

00:22:02.215 --> 00:22:05.320
if we take something up
to the speed of light

00:22:05.320 --> 00:22:08.100
and it can survive
going the speed of light

00:22:08.100 --> 00:22:11.960
then it has to be able to
protect whatever's inside it,

00:22:11.960 --> 00:22:14.933
to also match the speed of light.

00:22:16.690 --> 00:22:17.523
Remember that

00:22:17.523 --> 00:22:19.210
as you get closer and closer
to the speed of light,

00:22:19.210 --> 00:22:20.900
and once you reach the speed of light,

00:22:20.900 --> 00:22:24.483
you cannot have mass anymore,
you are energy by definition.

00:22:25.460 --> 00:22:28.700
The way to get up to the limit

00:22:28.700 --> 00:22:32.070
is to have really good strong
shielding of whatever's there

00:22:32.070 --> 00:22:34.314
but then getting past it will require--

00:22:34.314 --> 00:22:35.860
- Shielding from what?

00:22:35.860 --> 00:22:37.100
- Interstellar particles for example.

00:22:37.100 --> 00:22:38.710
- Okay, stuff that could slam into you.

00:22:38.710 --> 00:22:40.040
- Yeah, stuff that hits you.

00:22:40.040 --> 00:22:40.873
- If something hit you
at the speed of light,

00:22:40.873 --> 00:22:41.706
that would hurt.

00:22:41.706 --> 00:22:43.164
- Yeah, a lot.
- Yeah, okay.

00:22:43.164 --> 00:22:44.493
- And that's just light.
- And that's just light.

00:22:44.493 --> 00:22:46.793
Then it just feels good.
- Ow, ow, ow.

00:22:48.130 --> 00:22:50.550
- That would be funny, you
go to the beach, ouch, oouch.

00:22:50.550 --> 00:22:52.640
(laughing)

00:22:52.640 --> 00:22:54.119
That's what the sunscreen is for!

00:22:54.119 --> 00:22:56.950
- That's what it is.
- And it's literally a screen.

00:22:56.950 --> 00:22:58.003
- Or a force field.

00:22:58.860 --> 00:23:02.860
- You would have to find a
way for this vehicle itself

00:23:02.860 --> 00:23:06.030
to survive and by extension
whatever's inside the vehicle

00:23:06.030 --> 00:23:07.300
would survive as well.

00:23:07.300 --> 00:23:08.780
Anything piggyback though

00:23:08.780 --> 00:23:09.930
probably would be burnt to a crisp.

00:23:09.930 --> 00:23:11.400
- But I wanna make something clear,

00:23:11.400 --> 00:23:16.400
that speed does not kill,
it's acceleration that kills.

00:23:17.620 --> 00:23:18.453
- Right.

00:23:18.453 --> 00:23:22.110
- Okay, so you can be cruising
at 99.99% the speed of light.

00:23:22.110 --> 00:23:23.880
No one will know and no one will care

00:23:23.880 --> 00:23:25.925
but if you abruptly come to zero

00:23:25.925 --> 00:23:27.590
or if you abruptly speed up to that,

00:23:27.590 --> 00:23:30.108
then you're a pile of goo at
the back of the spaceship.

00:23:30.108 --> 00:23:32.630
- So if you could be magicked
into the middle of something

00:23:32.630 --> 00:23:34.450
that was already moving
at the speed of light

00:23:34.450 --> 00:23:36.930
or at 99% of the speed of light

00:23:38.770 --> 00:23:41.590
- Well, you could accelerate
there slowly, you can do that,

00:23:41.590 --> 00:23:45.150
in fact, they have spaceships
we've, we, I mean people,

00:23:45.150 --> 00:23:46.810
engineers have conceived of spaceships

00:23:46.810 --> 00:23:49.790
that accelerate at one
G, one Earth gravity

00:23:49.790 --> 00:23:51.350
and then it just feels like
you're just sitting here

00:23:51.350 --> 00:23:52.580
having this conversation

00:23:52.580 --> 00:23:54.730
but we're going faster
and faster and faster.

00:23:54.730 --> 00:23:56.310
Do you remember how quickly
you get to the speed of light,

00:23:56.310 --> 00:23:58.490
it's pretty, like six months or something?

00:23:58.490 --> 00:23:59.627
- It's not that bad.
- [Neil] It's not that bad.

00:23:59.627 --> 00:24:01.925
- Within a year.
- [Neil] Within a year.

00:24:01.925 --> 00:24:02.890
- But then doesn't--

00:24:02.890 --> 00:24:04.717
- Then you have to slow
down, it also takes--

00:24:04.717 --> 00:24:06.050
- You just turn the ship around

00:24:06.050 --> 00:24:08.010
and slow down in that
direction, so you still.

00:24:08.010 --> 00:24:08.843
- Right.

00:24:08.843 --> 00:24:11.920
- But doesn't your mass then increase as

00:24:11.920 --> 00:24:15.890
- Your mass increases relative
to other things around you.

00:24:15.890 --> 00:24:17.996
- Okay, so within the spaceship

00:24:17.996 --> 00:24:18.829
that's also moving
- You're fine.

00:24:18.829 --> 00:24:19.662
- at that speed.

00:24:19.662 --> 00:24:22.960
- Right, so you and I would
maintain our relative masses

00:24:22.960 --> 00:24:26.140
but you and I would not
maintain our relative masses

00:24:26.140 --> 00:24:29.421
compared to Zorcon who's
out next to the ship.

00:24:29.421 --> 00:24:30.254
- Oh, that Zorcon.
- It would be nice,

00:24:30.254 --> 00:24:31.270
he weighs like 108 pounds.

00:24:31.270 --> 00:24:32.553
(laughing)

00:24:32.553 --> 00:24:33.595
He's tryin' to gain some mass here.

00:24:33.595 --> 00:24:35.063
(laughing)

00:24:35.063 --> 00:24:37.530
- How much faster do I
have to run than Zorcon to

00:24:39.290 --> 00:24:40.130
look beach ready,

00:24:40.130 --> 00:24:40.963
- There you go.

00:24:40.963 --> 00:24:42.510
- As the sun was coming up?

00:24:42.510 --> 00:24:44.610
While we're talking about
summer and atmosphere

00:24:44.610 --> 00:24:49.487
and such like, Times
Natural on Instagram asked,

00:24:49.487 --> 00:24:51.666
"If Mars were to be terraformed

00:24:51.666 --> 00:24:53.917
"thus given an Earth-like atmosphere,

00:24:53.917 --> 00:24:56.047
"how would it be possible
to keep the atmosphere

00:24:56.047 --> 00:24:58.427
"from being blown away by solar winds

00:24:58.427 --> 00:25:00.855
"considering Mars does not
have the magnetic protection

00:25:00.855 --> 00:25:02.230
"that we do?"
- No magnetic shield.

00:25:02.230 --> 00:25:03.558
- These are good questions, dude!

00:25:03.558 --> 00:25:05.679
- That's an excellent question!

00:25:05.679 --> 00:25:10.230
- I think the first question
which is even more important

00:25:10.230 --> 00:25:12.650
is Mars doesn't have the gravity

00:25:12.650 --> 00:25:14.897
to hold on to our atmosphere.

00:25:14.897 --> 00:25:17.413
If we tried to put as
much atmosphere on Earth,

00:25:17.413 --> 00:25:20.300
the tops of it would continuously

00:25:20.300 --> 00:25:22.960
be going away before we
could develop enough pressure

00:25:22.960 --> 00:25:26.504
on the surface to get to say,
Earth's atmospheric pressure.

00:25:26.504 --> 00:25:30.683
The answer is it just has to
be constantly being generated.

00:25:31.913 --> 00:25:34.270
- But wait Charles, there's
clearly evidence of it

00:25:34.270 --> 00:25:37.290
once having had running
water on it's surface.

00:25:37.290 --> 00:25:39.475
So there had to be enough air pressure

00:25:39.475 --> 00:25:42.050
to prevent the water from just evaporating

00:25:42.050 --> 00:25:43.260
and that had to have been sustained

00:25:43.260 --> 00:25:45.230
for some fair amount of time

00:25:45.230 --> 00:25:47.420
'cause some of the rivers are meandering

00:25:47.420 --> 00:25:50.380
and that's a slow thing, you
don't just get that for free.

00:25:50.380 --> 00:25:53.350
- We're talking about
millions of years, right?

00:25:53.350 --> 00:25:55.570
Maybe even up to a billion years.

00:25:55.570 --> 00:25:56.510
When Mars,

00:25:56.510 --> 00:25:59.210
like any other planet in our
Solar System was forming,

00:25:59.210 --> 00:26:03.130
the core was slowly
aggregating it's metals

00:26:03.130 --> 00:26:04.220
from the surface.

00:26:04.220 --> 00:26:06.720
It's a differentiation
process where, in the same way

00:26:06.720 --> 00:26:09.550
that pulp in orange juice
sinks to the bottom,

00:26:09.550 --> 00:26:12.247
so too is the metals in the rock--

00:26:12.247 --> 00:26:13.920
- Wait, does pulp sink to the bottom?

00:26:13.920 --> 00:26:15.520
- Eventually.
- My pulp floats.

00:26:16.400 --> 00:26:20.067
- Oh, give it time.
- What's with your pulp?

00:26:20.067 --> 00:26:21.260
- Okay, I got floaty pulp.

00:26:21.260 --> 00:26:24.120
- Wow, I wanna drink your orange juice.

00:26:24.120 --> 00:26:26.370
(laughing)

00:26:28.009 --> 00:26:29.530
- Freshly squeezed orange
juice will actually separate.

00:26:29.530 --> 00:26:31.800
- That's right, the
separation of the orange juice

00:26:31.800 --> 00:26:35.460
is an analogy to the
separation of a metal core

00:26:35.460 --> 00:26:38.500
and a rocky mantel and then
the rocky crust on the outside.

00:26:38.500 --> 00:26:39.670
- We think of rocks as heavy

00:26:39.670 --> 00:26:42.140
but they're light compared
to metal, so they float.

00:26:42.140 --> 00:26:43.920
- Okay, that makes sense.
- When the metal sinks

00:26:43.920 --> 00:26:47.060
heat is dissipated outward,

00:26:47.060 --> 00:26:52.060
gently but with vigor
into the surroundings,

00:26:52.200 --> 00:26:54.040
the crust and eventually the atmosphere.

00:26:54.040 --> 00:26:56.900
In that process, you can
get a lot of outgassing

00:26:56.900 --> 00:27:00.630
from underneath the surface
out onto the surface

00:27:00.630 --> 00:27:03.330
which means that you can
for many millions of years

00:27:03.330 --> 00:27:04.163
- While it's happening.

00:27:04.163 --> 00:27:06.270
- While it's happening,
sustain the atmosphere

00:27:06.270 --> 00:27:09.600
that will create meandering
rivers that you describe.

00:27:09.600 --> 00:27:10.770
I believe that--

00:27:10.770 --> 00:27:12.648
- It doesn't take a million
years to meander a river.

00:27:12.648 --> 00:27:14.071
(laughing)

00:27:14.071 --> 00:27:16.832
- It may take that much time to say,

00:27:16.832 --> 00:27:19.617
evolve a protovirus or something.

00:27:21.110 --> 00:27:24.150
The answer is the same answer

00:27:24.150 --> 00:27:27.730
that no, we cannot prevent
it from going away,

00:27:27.730 --> 00:27:30.960
either because of magnetic
fields not being there

00:27:30.960 --> 00:27:33.320
nor by not enough gravity.

00:27:33.320 --> 00:27:35.710
Instead we just have to keep generating

00:27:35.710 --> 00:27:38.260
more and more atmosphere at the surface

00:27:38.260 --> 00:27:40.440
so that we can maintain equilibrium

00:27:40.440 --> 00:27:42.283
even as the outer layers are going away.

00:27:42.283 --> 00:27:44.610
- Just to be clear, the
magnetic field shields us

00:27:44.610 --> 00:27:45.930
from particles from the sun

00:27:45.930 --> 00:27:47.200
that would otherwise

00:27:47.200 --> 00:27:49.840
basically pick off the
faster moving atoms.

00:27:49.840 --> 00:27:52.030
- Would there be other
problems being on Mars

00:27:52.030 --> 00:27:53.520
without that shielding,
- Oh yes, oh yes.

00:27:53.520 --> 00:27:55.630
- just with the solar
winds hitting you and.

00:27:55.630 --> 00:28:00.630
- It's not just magnetic
field, isn't it also the ozone?

00:28:01.940 --> 00:28:06.940
Because UV from the sun will
break apart the water molecule

00:28:07.230 --> 00:28:11.130
and make it H2O and the H is
hydrogen, the lightest element

00:28:11.130 --> 00:28:13.890
and it moves very fast in
any thermal environment,

00:28:13.890 --> 00:28:17.440
you lose that, stranding
the oxygen behind.

00:28:17.440 --> 00:28:18.770
So you systematically

00:28:18.770 --> 00:28:21.250
molecule by molecule take out your water.

00:28:21.250 --> 00:28:23.860
And that had nothing to do
with the magnetic field.

00:28:23.860 --> 00:28:26.260
- Yeah, there's all kinds of problems.

00:28:26.260 --> 00:28:28.580
When you don't have a protection

00:28:28.580 --> 00:28:30.990
against ionizing radiation of any kind,

00:28:30.990 --> 00:28:33.654
whether they be light,
like gamma rays and Xrays,

00:28:33.654 --> 00:28:36.380
or whether they be charged particles.

00:28:36.380 --> 00:28:38.297
- Let's just go out and say,

00:28:38.297 --> 00:28:41.460
"If you can terraform Mars,
you can put up a shield".

00:28:41.460 --> 00:28:42.413
Just think about it!

00:28:42.413 --> 00:28:44.950
- Or just a big magnet, how big a magnet.

00:28:44.950 --> 00:28:46.780
- Thank you, one of those
little cartoon magnets.

00:28:46.780 --> 00:28:48.725
- There you go (magnet attracting)

00:28:48.725 --> 00:28:50.170
- With the zaggy lines on it.

00:28:50.170 --> 00:28:51.360
- I would love to have that.

00:28:51.360 --> 00:28:52.920
- Everyone has their own magnet

00:28:52.920 --> 00:28:55.320
that they walk around
with like an umbrella.

00:28:55.320 --> 00:28:56.800
- Their own magnet!?

00:28:56.800 --> 00:28:59.789
On a miner's helmet, they
got their own magnet.

00:28:59.789 --> 00:29:00.910
(laughing)

00:29:00.910 --> 00:29:02.460
The engineers will find a
solution to that, Charles.

00:29:02.460 --> 00:29:04.510
- No one can stand too close to a fridge.

00:29:05.525 --> 00:29:07.810
- I think the terraforming reality

00:29:07.810 --> 00:29:09.800
is that you are gonna
have to live in domes

00:29:09.800 --> 00:29:10.850
in a place like Mars.

00:29:10.850 --> 00:29:13.199
You can't just leave it open.

00:29:13.199 --> 00:29:15.710
- That would be funny if they
had a magnet on the helmet.

00:29:15.710 --> 00:29:17.864
Get to close to your
refrigerator, you just get stuck.

00:29:17.864 --> 00:29:19.260
Mommy! (laughing)

00:29:19.260 --> 00:29:20.750
It's like the modern version
of getting your tongue stuck

00:29:20.750 --> 00:29:24.275
on the frozen pipe.
(laughing)

00:29:24.275 --> 00:29:25.675
- Yeah, that would be funny.

00:29:27.610 --> 00:29:28.443
- Which I think as a scientist
you know if that happens,

00:29:28.443 --> 00:29:31.290
the best thing to do is just
keep pulling, just keep.

00:29:31.290 --> 00:29:32.267
lean back as hard

00:29:32.267 --> 00:29:33.310
(tongue stretching)

00:29:33.310 --> 00:29:34.677
(laughing)

00:29:34.677 --> 00:29:36.227
and then eventually it
will snap and then roll up.

00:29:36.227 --> 00:29:38.030
- It'll roll up, yes.

00:29:38.030 --> 00:29:39.350
- And that's how it works,

00:29:39.350 --> 00:29:41.303
we heard it here from two scientists.

00:29:42.280 --> 00:29:43.980
Well, we've got scientists here as well,

00:29:43.980 --> 00:29:47.947
OneHive Gazette on Patreon asks,

00:29:47.947 --> 00:29:50.517
"I've heard there are benefits
to putting a space telescope

00:29:50.517 --> 00:29:52.247
"on the far side of the Moon.

00:29:52.247 --> 00:29:54.247
"I think because Earth's radio emissions

00:29:54.247 --> 00:29:55.787
"won't cause interference there.

00:29:55.787 --> 00:29:56.787
"Do you think there will eventually

00:29:56.787 --> 00:29:58.377
"be a telescope station there?

00:29:58.377 --> 00:30:00.367
"What exactly would be the benefits?"

00:30:01.380 --> 00:30:02.560
There's a name at the end of that,

00:30:02.560 --> 00:30:05.250
that's Patrick Follis from Mill Creek.

00:30:05.250 --> 00:30:09.120
- Well, Patrick, yes there
should be a telescope there

00:30:09.120 --> 00:30:11.150
and there are many benefits.

00:30:11.150 --> 00:30:12.940
- I attended an entire conference on this

00:30:12.940 --> 00:30:15.060
in Arizona a few years ago.
- Oh really, Oooo!

00:30:15.060 --> 00:30:16.710
- Astronomy from the far side of the Moon.

00:30:16.710 --> 00:30:19.400
And it was a delight just to see,

00:30:19.400 --> 00:30:22.160
because we're nowhere near getting there

00:30:22.160 --> 00:30:24.720
but to see these early
ideas of why it's useful.

00:30:24.720 --> 00:30:27.430
We are a radio noisy place

00:30:27.430 --> 00:30:31.380
and radio telescopes
need this radio silence.

00:30:31.380 --> 00:30:35.566
It's basically noise in
the traditional sense.

00:30:35.566 --> 00:30:37.620
It's electromagnetic noise,

00:30:37.620 --> 00:30:40.430
noise signals that you
don't want to be there

00:30:40.430 --> 00:30:41.570
'cause you're trying to detect something

00:30:41.570 --> 00:30:42.972
that's dimmer than--

00:30:42.972 --> 00:30:44.280
- And would it all be blocked out

00:30:44.280 --> 00:30:45.150
by being on the other side of the Moon?

00:30:45.150 --> 00:30:46.320
- Essentially yeah,

00:30:46.320 --> 00:30:48.830
it won't have to penetrate
through the Moon.

00:30:48.830 --> 00:30:50.570
- There aren't waves that can go around?

00:30:50.570 --> 00:30:51.845
- There is some defraction.

00:30:51.845 --> 00:30:53.100
- A little bit but on the edges

00:30:53.100 --> 00:30:55.570
but if you're smack dab on
the other side of the Moon,

00:30:55.570 --> 00:30:56.720
Earth is dead to you.

00:30:56.720 --> 00:30:58.600
- You should be able to avoid it.

00:30:58.600 --> 00:31:00.070
For example, space craft now

00:31:00.070 --> 00:31:02.764
which have landed on
the far side of the Moon

00:31:02.764 --> 00:31:04.750
can communicate with Earth

00:31:04.750 --> 00:31:08.933
because there is a satellite
in orbit around the Moon.

00:31:08.933 --> 00:31:10.650
- Oh, so that sort of bounces it off.

00:31:10.650 --> 00:31:12.730
- It bounces it off.
- [Neil] Exactly.

00:31:12.730 --> 00:31:14.170
So any civilization,

00:31:14.170 --> 00:31:17.227
any colony would have to have

00:31:17.227 --> 00:31:20.730
some kind of way to bounce
off of the side of the Moon

00:31:20.730 --> 00:31:22.110
to come straight back to us,

00:31:22.110 --> 00:31:23.840
either by permanent stations

00:31:23.840 --> 00:31:28.020
like towers that can talk
to each other, by the way--

00:31:28.020 --> 00:31:30.640
- It's like those look behind
glasses that spies have.

00:31:30.640 --> 00:31:31.473
- Yes.

00:31:31.473 --> 00:31:32.450
- That you get from the back of magazines.

00:31:32.450 --> 00:31:33.575
- Whatever happened to those?

00:31:33.575 --> 00:31:35.200
- Oh, Spiderman still uses them.

00:31:35.200 --> 00:31:36.033
- Really?

00:31:36.033 --> 00:31:37.970
- According to
- He doesn't use Spidey-sense?

00:31:37.970 --> 00:31:40.560
He's gotta use Google
Glasses to see behind him?

00:31:40.560 --> 00:31:44.020
- According to information
from decades ago

00:31:44.020 --> 00:31:48.238
which may have been outpaced
by 21st century technology,

00:31:48.238 --> 00:31:52.400
Spiderman's mask doesn't
actually have eye holes

00:31:52.400 --> 00:31:55.441
and he actually sees through
mirrors that's off to the side.

00:31:55.441 --> 00:31:57.370
- Really, I didn't know that.

00:31:57.370 --> 00:31:58.403
- I do also like to think

00:31:58.403 --> 00:32:00.120
that all spies still buy their stuff

00:32:00.120 --> 00:32:01.609
from the back of joke magazines.

00:32:01.609 --> 00:32:02.870
- Absolutely.

00:32:02.870 --> 00:32:04.260
- That's where they get
their invisible ink.

00:32:04.260 --> 00:32:08.440
- That's how I got my Charles
Atlas body in seven days.

00:32:08.440 --> 00:32:09.603
- And it is remarkable!

00:32:10.530 --> 00:32:13.030
- And the Xray specs,
you wanna see the bones,

00:32:13.030 --> 00:32:14.370
the people's bones.

00:32:14.370 --> 00:32:16.200
- Which would also enable
you to see through the Moon

00:32:16.200 --> 00:32:19.110
to Earth, so you wouldn't
have any of that problem.

00:32:19.110 --> 00:32:20.330
- Yeah, so it's a great idea,

00:32:20.330 --> 00:32:21.930
especially for radio telescopes.

00:32:23.370 --> 00:32:26.750
- Radio telescopes especially are big

00:32:26.750 --> 00:32:27.920
and that's one of the reasons

00:32:27.920 --> 00:32:29.830
why you can't just send
them out into space

00:32:29.830 --> 00:32:33.637
far enough away from Earth to
not be affected by the radio

00:32:33.637 --> 00:32:34.670
- That was gonna be my next question,

00:32:34.670 --> 00:32:37.010
is why you can't just
put it on a Moon lander

00:32:37.010 --> 00:32:38.730
and then drive it across to the far side?

00:32:38.730 --> 00:32:40.300
- I mean, radio telescopes are big, right?

00:32:40.300 --> 00:32:43.140
If you remember the movie,
Contact, Jodie Foster

00:32:43.140 --> 00:32:45.660
is sitting in just one antenna

00:32:45.660 --> 00:32:47.950
of the very large array in New Mexico.

00:32:47.950 --> 00:32:48.783
- Okay.

00:32:48.783 --> 00:32:51.760
- So, that single antenna
is much larger than--

00:32:51.760 --> 00:32:54.510
- She wasn't in the antenna,
she was at the base of it.

00:32:55.520 --> 00:32:57.256
Well, she was at Arecibo telescope.

00:32:57.256 --> 00:32:59.510
- [Charles] That's right
and she was in that.

00:32:59.510 --> 00:33:00.780
- Close to the edge of it.

00:33:00.780 --> 00:33:02.380
Yeah, radio telescopes are big.

00:33:02.380 --> 00:33:04.730
They're big 'cause radio waves are big

00:33:04.730 --> 00:33:07.380
and to get a lot of radio waves,

00:33:07.380 --> 00:33:09.680
to focus them you need a bigger dish

00:33:09.680 --> 00:33:11.480
than you'd otherwise think you need.

00:33:12.691 --> 00:33:15.750
Since the far side also gets sunlight,

00:33:15.750 --> 00:33:17.760
it's not the dark side of the moon.

00:33:17.760 --> 00:33:19.400
It's not like you're in eternal darkness

00:33:19.400 --> 00:33:21.650
which might be an astronomers dream state

00:33:21.650 --> 00:33:24.752
but a day on the Moon lasts a moonth.

00:33:24.752 --> 00:33:25.585
(laughing)

00:33:25.585 --> 00:33:27.370
All sides get sunlight.

00:33:27.370 --> 00:33:29.697
15 days of darkness, 15 days of sunlight.

00:33:29.697 --> 00:33:32.640
- And radio astronomers can
actually work in the daytime.

00:33:32.640 --> 00:33:33.800
Although.
- [Neil] Which is one of the

00:33:33.800 --> 00:33:34.750
coolest things ever!

00:33:34.750 --> 00:33:35.900
- Which is really really cool,

00:33:35.900 --> 00:33:39.850
although the sun is a big
source of radio noise.

00:33:39.850 --> 00:33:41.350
There are a lot of trade
offs back and forth

00:33:41.350 --> 00:33:42.630
but it's a really cool--

00:33:42.630 --> 00:33:44.920
- There's a zone around the
sun, you wanna keep clear of it

00:33:44.920 --> 00:33:46.650
'cause it'll be too noisy for you.

00:33:46.650 --> 00:33:47.620
It'll be worse than Earth.

00:33:47.620 --> 00:33:50.240
- Where would the best
place to be a telescope?

00:33:50.240 --> 00:33:51.140
- On the Moon?

00:33:51.140 --> 00:33:52.290
- Yeah, or anywhere.

00:33:52.290 --> 00:33:54.040
If you could put a telescope
anywhere in the Universe,

00:33:54.040 --> 00:33:55.818
where would you put it?

00:33:55.818 --> 00:33:56.810
- Oooo.
- [Charles] Wow.

00:33:56.810 --> 00:33:58.319
- [Matt] And that question
- If you could do that,

00:33:58.319 --> 00:34:00.264
why do you need a telescope?
- comes from Matt Kirshen in.

00:34:00.264 --> 00:34:02.370
- Just go to the place you're
looking at with the telescope.

00:34:02.370 --> 00:34:03.492
- All right, well that's a fair point.

00:34:03.492 --> 00:34:04.677
(laughing)

00:34:04.677 --> 00:34:05.830
Just go there with a moped.

00:34:05.830 --> 00:34:07.980
- If you could put a
telescope between galaxies,

00:34:07.980 --> 00:34:09.530
where it's dark and there's nothing

00:34:09.530 --> 00:34:11.560
then just go to where you're looking.

00:34:11.560 --> 00:34:12.393
- Right.

00:34:12.393 --> 00:34:14.650
- But don't you sometimes
want the wider view?

00:34:14.650 --> 00:34:17.883
- The comfort of sitting back
and being able to re-point.

00:34:18.759 --> 00:34:22.900
- I would be more excited
about getting a big telescope

00:34:22.900 --> 00:34:26.180
then putting the telescope
in any given location.

00:34:26.180 --> 00:34:27.013
If we can get a telescope

00:34:27.013 --> 00:34:28.580
that's the size of the Solar System

00:34:28.580 --> 00:34:30.810
or the size of the Milky Way galaxy,

00:34:30.810 --> 00:34:33.570
now we could put that anywhere
that it doesn't get broken

00:34:33.570 --> 00:34:34.710
and I'm totally cool with that.

00:34:34.710 --> 00:34:36.100
- Yeah, that's a good point.

00:34:36.100 --> 00:34:38.580
You can make much bigger
structures in space,

00:34:38.580 --> 00:34:40.460
bigger than Earth itself

00:34:40.460 --> 00:34:42.620
and then you got the baddest
ass telescope there ever was.

00:34:42.620 --> 00:34:43.743
- So that would be another
use for once you get the

00:34:43.743 --> 00:34:46.470
technology to start
building stuff with robots.

00:34:46.470 --> 00:34:49.030
- Ah, yes.
- Exactly.

00:34:49.030 --> 00:34:50.260
- Well, is the first thing we build,

00:34:50.260 --> 00:34:53.730
with a robot in space using
asteroids, a telescope?

00:34:53.730 --> 00:34:54.880
- I would.

00:34:54.880 --> 00:34:55.740
- Yeah!

00:34:55.740 --> 00:34:58.310
- But there's probably not
an economic driver for that.

00:34:58.310 --> 00:34:59.240
- Probably.

00:34:59.240 --> 00:35:02.197
- This question comes from
MozAmu on Instagram who says,

00:35:02.197 --> 00:35:04.877
"How would you market", that's in quotes,

00:35:04.877 --> 00:35:06.727
"space exploration to someone

00:35:06.727 --> 00:35:09.317
"with an extremely utilitarian view.

00:35:09.317 --> 00:35:11.317
"I'm definitely not the
one who needs convincing

00:35:11.317 --> 00:35:14.160
"but that type of criticism
is abundant nowadays"

00:35:14.160 --> 00:35:16.520
and then also, "Lots of love
from your fans in Dubai".

00:35:16.520 --> 00:35:18.750
- Dubai, very interesting!
- Wow!

00:35:18.750 --> 00:35:21.500
Cool, well, the answer to
that is, we don't have time.

00:35:21.500 --> 00:35:25.350
We're gonna take a break
and when we come back,

00:35:25.350 --> 00:35:26.183
we're gonna find out

00:35:26.183 --> 00:35:30.840
how to convert the non-believers
in space exploration

00:35:30.840 --> 00:35:32.243
when StarTalk returns.

00:35:34.110 --> 00:35:35.881
We're back on StarTalk.

00:35:35.881 --> 00:35:38.300
Tyson here, Charles Liu there.

00:35:38.300 --> 00:35:39.133
- Hello.

00:35:39.133 --> 00:35:41.460
- [Neil] Matt Kirshen there and

00:35:41.460 --> 00:35:44.900
we are commemorating Space
Week and just to remind people

00:35:44.900 --> 00:35:46.670
in case you didn't know,
the first week of October

00:35:46.670 --> 00:35:51.384
happened to have been the week

00:35:51.384 --> 00:35:54.170
that Sputnik was launched.

00:35:54.170 --> 00:35:57.236
October 4th, 1957

00:35:57.236 --> 00:35:59.800
by the Russians.

00:35:59.800 --> 00:36:03.840
Soviet Union, our sworn
enemy, the Godless Communists

00:36:03.840 --> 00:36:07.640
and we lost our shit (laughing) over here.

00:36:07.640 --> 00:36:10.000
It would take us a year,

00:36:10.000 --> 00:36:13.310
but a year later in the
first week of October,

00:36:13.310 --> 00:36:15.450
we would found NASA.

00:36:15.450 --> 00:36:18.900
NASA would become an agency a year later.

00:36:18.900 --> 00:36:21.610
So, those two events
both happened in October

00:36:21.610 --> 00:36:23.410
as well as many other space missions

00:36:23.410 --> 00:36:24.810
that have occurred in October

00:36:24.810 --> 00:36:28.440
have made a perfectly good
argument to call this Space Week.

00:36:28.440 --> 00:36:29.940
- I think it's great.

00:36:29.940 --> 00:36:32.400
- We are Cosmic Queries,
so keep going Matt.

00:36:32.400 --> 00:36:34.487
- So, this is the question
from just before the break is

00:36:34.487 --> 00:36:36.430
"How would you market space exploration

00:36:36.430 --> 00:36:37.263
- [Neil] To non-believers

00:36:37.263 --> 00:36:39.570
- "To someone with an
extremely utilitarian view".

00:36:39.570 --> 00:36:41.353
- Yeah, Charles, what would you do?

00:36:41.353 --> 00:36:43.362
You're an educator,
scientist-based enthusiast,

00:36:43.362 --> 00:36:44.385
what would you do?

00:36:44.385 --> 00:36:46.690
- Well, I have to use
many different strategies

00:36:46.690 --> 00:36:47.523
with my students.

00:36:47.523 --> 00:36:50.080
I often teach introductory
astronomy classes

00:36:50.080 --> 00:36:53.000
where most of the students are
not gonna become scientists.

00:36:53.000 --> 00:36:53.833
- [Neil] Okay.

00:36:53.833 --> 00:36:54.970
- So how do you convince them

00:36:54.970 --> 00:36:56.950
that it's a good idea
to go out into space?

00:36:56.950 --> 00:36:58.020
I think there's a very good,

00:36:58.020 --> 00:37:00.800
and you have to use many
different strategies.

00:37:00.800 --> 00:37:03.610
One strategy being in the utilitarian side

00:37:03.610 --> 00:37:07.930
is to convince people that
profit can be made, right?

00:37:07.930 --> 00:37:12.930
The same way that people came
to North America from Europe.

00:37:14.060 --> 00:37:14.893
- [Neil] The new world.

00:37:14.893 --> 00:37:16.530
- Yeah, back in the 1600s

00:37:16.530 --> 00:37:19.650
was precisely people convincing others

00:37:19.650 --> 00:37:21.560
that if you send me to America,

00:37:21.560 --> 00:37:23.950
I can make money for you
and send back stuff for you

00:37:23.950 --> 00:37:27.580
whether it's gold,
whether it's beaver pelts,

00:37:27.580 --> 00:37:29.620
whether it's agricultural
goods, things like that.

00:37:29.620 --> 00:37:31.400
- I can name places after you.

00:37:31.400 --> 00:37:33.730
- For example, yes.
- [Neil] (laughing)

00:37:33.730 --> 00:37:34.563
They weren't very creative,

00:37:34.563 --> 00:37:35.913
they all began with the word 'new'.

00:37:35.913 --> 00:37:37.080
(laughing)

00:37:37.080 --> 00:37:40.637
New Haven, New York, New everything is

00:37:40.637 --> 00:37:41.790
- New England,
- New England.

00:37:41.790 --> 00:37:43.300
- it's just the whole place.

00:37:43.300 --> 00:37:46.200
Profit is a utilitarian thing you can do.

00:37:46.200 --> 00:37:51.200
Another thing is to appeal
to their sense of grandeur,

00:37:51.490 --> 00:37:54.207
grandiosity, vanity if you will.

00:37:54.207 --> 00:37:55.650
- [Neil] Ego.
- Ego, yeah.

00:37:55.650 --> 00:37:59.797
And say, "If you go out
there, you could be the person

00:37:59.797 --> 00:38:02.877
"that everyone will remember
for all of human history."

00:38:04.260 --> 00:38:06.050
A lot of times people will ask me that

00:38:06.050 --> 00:38:07.130
and younger kids for example

00:38:07.130 --> 00:38:09.330
will ask me some scientific question

00:38:09.330 --> 00:38:11.356
which we don't know the answer to.

00:38:11.356 --> 00:38:13.260
There are many of those.

00:38:13.260 --> 00:38:14.587
I will say something along the lines of,

00:38:14.587 --> 00:38:16.717
"You know what, nobody knows yet.

00:38:16.717 --> 00:38:19.117
"Maybe someday you will be
the person that discovers that

00:38:19.117 --> 00:38:21.707
"and then we will all remember you

00:38:21.707 --> 00:38:23.890
"and your scientific discovery".

00:38:23.890 --> 00:38:25.440
- I have a better answer.

00:38:25.440 --> 00:38:28.240
Here's what you do, you go
to that person who's the non,

00:38:29.470 --> 00:38:33.130
okay go to that person
and in the dark of night,

00:38:33.130 --> 00:38:34.460
sneak into their place.

00:38:34.460 --> 00:38:37.440
- Oh yeah, that's real
socially acceptable.

00:38:38.580 --> 00:38:40.725
- You do this with a search warrant,

00:38:40.725 --> 00:38:44.300
then you remove everything in their home

00:38:44.300 --> 00:38:46.440
that was inspired or developed

00:38:46.440 --> 00:38:48.800
because of space technologies.

00:38:48.800 --> 00:38:51.010
So first you take away the smartphone

00:38:51.010 --> 00:38:54.380
and then you take away
all integrated circuits

00:38:54.380 --> 00:38:57.510
and all of this, take
away their Uber account,

00:38:57.510 --> 00:39:01.300
take away their health,
their Lasik surgery

00:39:01.300 --> 00:39:02.640
- Non-stick pans.

00:39:02.640 --> 00:39:03.700
- [Neil] All of this.

00:39:03.700 --> 00:39:05.446
- The pens that write upside down.

00:39:05.446 --> 00:39:06.875
(laughing)

00:39:06.875 --> 00:39:09.137
- And then they wake up, and they say,

00:39:09.137 --> 00:39:11.487
"What the hell happened!?"

00:39:11.487 --> 00:39:16.487
And take away weather,
what's the weather channel

00:39:16.730 --> 00:39:18.550
Take away all the weather forecasting,

00:39:18.550 --> 00:39:22.023
take away it all and then just
leave them there bare-assed.

00:39:23.500 --> 00:39:24.920
So then they'll learn quickly

00:39:24.920 --> 00:39:26.050
what role space has played in our lives.

00:39:26.050 --> 00:39:31.050
- You really can say that space
technology, basic science,

00:39:31.770 --> 00:39:33.300
those kinds of things,

00:39:33.300 --> 00:39:37.657
that do not change the price
of bread today can and have

00:39:37.657 --> 00:39:40.680
changed the course of
civilization tomorrow.

00:39:40.680 --> 00:39:42.070
And we wanna be part of it.
- Wait, stop there.

00:39:42.070 --> 00:39:44.463
Don't add words to that, that was perfect.

00:39:44.463 --> 00:39:46.750
Just the way it came out of your mouth.

00:39:46.750 --> 00:39:47.700
- Print it on a t-shirt.

00:39:47.700 --> 00:39:50.240
- You gotta know when to
stop the beautiful quote.

00:39:50.240 --> 00:39:52.330
- It won't change the price of bread today

00:39:52.330 --> 00:39:55.930
but it could change the course
of civilization tomorrow.

00:39:55.930 --> 00:39:57.318
- Yes.

00:39:57.318 --> 00:39:58.151
(laughing)

00:39:58.151 --> 00:39:59.470
All right, Matt, next question.

00:39:59.470 --> 00:40:01.000
- Well, I'm gonna combine
these two questions

00:40:01.000 --> 00:40:01.833
because they are--

00:40:01.833 --> 00:40:02.950
- Is that allowed?

00:40:02.950 --> 00:40:03.783
Judges?

00:40:04.890 --> 00:40:06.281
We wanna hear who said them.

00:40:06.281 --> 00:40:08.800
- I absolutely will, I'll
credit both these people.

00:40:08.800 --> 00:40:11.231
It's a continuation of
what we were talking about.

00:40:11.231 --> 00:40:15.557
Jared Decker asks, "In the
future, will it be government

00:40:15.557 --> 00:40:17.697
"or private sector or a
combination of the both

00:40:17.697 --> 00:40:20.920
"that will continue humanities
journey through space?"

00:40:20.920 --> 00:40:25.920
And then, Paul Pundear on Twitter asks,

00:40:26.237 --> 00:40:30.277
"As Neil Tyson, @neiltyson
stated in his book,

00:40:30.277 --> 00:40:33.157
"Military and science flourish together,

00:40:33.157 --> 00:40:36.957
"there are chances that the
next scientific breakthrough

00:40:36.957 --> 00:40:40.510
"happened but is classified
by the government."

00:40:40.510 --> 00:40:42.080
Thoughts?

00:40:42.080 --> 00:40:44.523
- Oh, well that's already happened.

00:40:45.420 --> 00:40:46.480
It's very true that--

00:40:46.480 --> 00:40:47.840
- Charles, we're not
authorized to go that far.

00:40:47.840 --> 00:40:50.210
- Oh, I'm sorry, you're right, sorry.

00:40:50.210 --> 00:40:52.630
- I'm just kidding (laughing)

00:40:52.630 --> 00:40:55.693
- No, it could never happen, no!

00:40:57.056 --> 00:40:59.297
- We are fine, knowledge
is out, we are happy.

00:40:59.297 --> 00:41:00.737
(laughing)

00:41:00.737 --> 00:41:03.010
- Neil has written very eloquently

00:41:03.010 --> 00:41:04.620
about this particular topic

00:41:04.620 --> 00:41:06.770
and so I'm gonna defer to
you in your conversation

00:41:06.770 --> 00:41:08.640
about the military aspect of things

00:41:08.640 --> 00:41:12.339
but I will say from my
purely civilian point of view

00:41:12.339 --> 00:41:16.590
to me the reason that the
military and the government

00:41:16.590 --> 00:41:19.930
and industry all work
together to move us forward

00:41:19.930 --> 00:41:22.480
in technology development and innovation,

00:41:22.480 --> 00:41:26.330
is simply because you
need a lot of resources

00:41:26.330 --> 00:41:29.000
in any society to do
those kinds of things.

00:41:29.000 --> 00:41:33.060
Innovation is almost never
just one guy in his basement.

00:41:33.060 --> 00:41:35.780
That's just not how we're gonna
move civilization forward.

00:41:35.780 --> 00:41:37.007
- But that's how you get
a movie made after you,

00:41:37.007 --> 00:41:39.900
if you're the one person
burning the midnight oil.

00:41:39.900 --> 00:41:40.770
- He's done it!

00:41:40.770 --> 00:41:42.870
- If it's 10 people taking five years

00:41:42.870 --> 00:41:45.140
in a lab funded by five different sources,

00:41:45.140 --> 00:41:46.182
nobody makes a movie about it.

00:41:46.182 --> 00:41:47.015
- I think that's right

00:41:47.015 --> 00:41:50.557
and our reality is as Neil has written,

00:41:50.557 --> 00:41:54.550
is that many many many hands are necessary

00:41:54.550 --> 00:41:57.180
and much much much money is needed

00:41:57.180 --> 00:42:01.100
to make those really
fundamental explorations.

00:42:01.100 --> 00:42:02.208
What do you think, Neil?

00:42:02.208 --> 00:42:07.050
- (laughing) Do you
know, Christiaan Huygens.

00:42:07.050 --> 00:42:07.883
- [Charles] Yes.

00:42:07.883 --> 00:42:09.800
- He's a Dutch polymath

00:42:11.440 --> 00:42:14.450
- He's credited with the rings
of Saturn, identifying them.

00:42:14.450 --> 00:42:15.631
- For understanding the rings.

00:42:15.631 --> 00:42:17.550
- Understanding that they are rings,

00:42:17.550 --> 00:42:20.050
he did a wave theory of light

00:42:20.050 --> 00:42:22.120
and he actually had a spacecraft,

00:42:22.120 --> 00:42:23.760
the Huygens probe named after him,

00:42:23.760 --> 00:42:25.990
the one that landed on
Saturn's moon Titan.

00:42:25.990 --> 00:42:26.940
- Detached from--

00:42:26.940 --> 00:42:31.140
- Yeah, beautiful and showed
those amazing pictures

00:42:31.140 --> 00:42:33.310
that showed that on Titan

00:42:33.310 --> 00:42:36.190
there are mountains and rivers and lakes

00:42:36.190 --> 00:42:37.290
just like we see on Earth

00:42:37.290 --> 00:42:39.800
but they're made of very
different substances.

00:42:39.800 --> 00:42:41.820
- They're made of methane.

00:42:41.820 --> 00:42:46.477
So, Huygens in one of
his books, hypothesizes

00:42:47.670 --> 00:42:51.440
that God put evil people on Earth

00:42:52.470 --> 00:42:57.470
so that good people would have to innovate

00:42:58.020 --> 00:42:59.850
and wage war against them

00:42:59.850 --> 00:43:03.510
by inventing new tools and technology

00:43:03.510 --> 00:43:06.250
and this is the source of civilization

00:43:06.250 --> 00:43:08.230
because we have bad people on Earth

00:43:08.230 --> 00:43:10.133
and this was part of God's plan.

00:43:10.990 --> 00:43:11.823
He writes this!

00:43:11.823 --> 00:43:13.091
- Wow, almost like--

00:43:13.091 --> 00:43:15.580
- This is how connected
and convinced he is

00:43:15.580 --> 00:43:18.060
that innovation and war go hand in hand.

00:43:18.060 --> 00:43:19.496
- Amazing.

00:43:19.496 --> 00:43:23.740
If you're just using military resources

00:43:23.740 --> 00:43:28.737
and then turning them to
purely discovery purposes,

00:43:28.737 --> 00:43:30.853
- [Neil] Exploration discovery.

00:43:30.853 --> 00:43:32.470
- then that's a different dynamic

00:43:32.470 --> 00:43:33.340
then what you're describing

00:43:33.340 --> 00:43:36.040
in terms of the actual desire to wage war.

00:43:36.040 --> 00:43:37.340
That's an excellent point.

00:43:38.360 --> 00:43:40.500
- And to the answer I
think, like Charles said

00:43:40.500 --> 00:43:41.480
it would be a combination,

00:43:41.480 --> 00:43:43.390
government will do some things first

00:43:43.390 --> 00:43:44.970
'cause there's not a profit motive yet

00:43:44.970 --> 00:43:47.390
until the trade winds are mapped

00:43:47.390 --> 00:43:49.930
and the hostiles and the
friendlies are established,

00:43:49.930 --> 00:43:51.760
then private enterprise can come in.

00:43:51.760 --> 00:43:52.950
I say this often,

00:43:52.950 --> 00:43:55.880
that the first Europeans to the new world

00:43:55.880 --> 00:43:58.070
were not the Dutch East
India Trading Company.

00:43:58.070 --> 00:44:00.928
It was governments who
took that first investment

00:44:00.928 --> 00:44:04.300
for hegemonistic regions
and economic reasons

00:44:04.300 --> 00:44:07.000
and then well, long-term economic reasons.

00:44:07.000 --> 00:44:09.200
Then once you know and it's mapped,

00:44:09.200 --> 00:44:11.100
this is how much it cost,
this is how long it took,

00:44:11.100 --> 00:44:12.320
this is what you can bring back.

00:44:12.320 --> 00:44:15.230
Now I can make a business
model, I can sell tickets

00:44:15.230 --> 00:44:18.250
and now we have the Dutch
East India Trading Company.

00:44:18.250 --> 00:44:20.130
- Right, that's a good point.

00:44:20.130 --> 00:44:21.280
- We gotta go into lightning round.

00:44:21.280 --> 00:44:22.560
- Uh oh.
- All right.

00:44:22.560 --> 00:44:23.780
- Lightning round reminder (ringing bell)

00:44:23.780 --> 00:44:26.610
Lightning round is sound bite answers.

00:44:26.610 --> 00:44:27.443
- Okay.
- All right.

00:44:27.443 --> 00:44:28.360
We'll see how good you've come,

00:44:28.360 --> 00:44:29.550
you've come a long way, Charles.

00:44:29.550 --> 00:44:30.383
- Oh, I don't know.

00:44:30.383 --> 00:44:31.600
- [Neil] All right, let's see, Matt, go!

00:44:31.600 --> 00:44:35.130
- All right, Corey Moon on
Twitter, no relation to our Moon

00:44:35.130 --> 00:44:39.617
says, "Has a nuclear bomb
ever been tested in space,

00:44:39.617 --> 00:44:41.350
"if so, what happened?"

00:44:41.350 --> 00:44:44.360
- Not to my knowledge,
but if it does happen

00:44:44.360 --> 00:44:46.820
then the radiation just
goes a long long way.

00:44:46.820 --> 00:44:50.430
- Half of why one would
use a nuclear weapon

00:44:50.430 --> 00:44:52.150
is for the blast wave

00:44:52.150 --> 00:44:55.400
that does most of the immediate damage.

00:44:55.400 --> 00:44:58.410
There are three ways atomic weapons work.

00:44:58.410 --> 00:45:02.480
One is the blast of light energy

00:45:02.480 --> 00:45:05.110
that will vaporize things on the spot.

00:45:05.110 --> 00:45:08.900
The next is the blast wave that
any typical bomb would have.

00:45:08.900 --> 00:45:10.660
The third is the radiation sickness

00:45:10.660 --> 00:45:12.248
that would then kill living things.

00:45:12.248 --> 00:45:15.560
In space, if there is no air,

00:45:15.560 --> 00:45:17.810
then the blast wave does not exist

00:45:17.810 --> 00:45:20.933
and half of why you would ever
use the bomb is nullified.

00:45:21.980 --> 00:45:23.030
- But you do get the radiation.

00:45:23.030 --> 00:45:24.157
- You do get the radiation

00:45:24.157 --> 00:45:26.180
and you get the light energy on top of it.

00:45:26.180 --> 00:45:27.941
- Which is also electromagnetic radiation.

00:45:27.941 --> 00:45:30.729
- I don't know if we've ever
exploded a bomb in space.

00:45:30.729 --> 00:45:31.617
(ringing bell)

00:45:31.617 --> 00:45:33.400
(laughing)

00:45:33.400 --> 00:45:35.687
- Thomas Kasner on Twitter says,

00:45:35.687 --> 00:45:38.270
"Star Trek included many
aliens and creatures

00:45:38.270 --> 00:45:39.750
that naturally existed in space

00:45:39.750 --> 00:45:43.067
outside of planetary
atmospheres, is that possible?"

00:45:43.950 --> 00:45:45.680
- Anything is possible.

00:45:45.680 --> 00:45:49.710
Can it be done with the DNA
structure that we're used to?

00:45:49.710 --> 00:45:51.030
Probably not.

00:45:51.030 --> 00:45:53.078
- I agree and think about it.

00:45:53.078 --> 00:45:58.078
Imagine you drive your
energy and your sustenance

00:45:58.260 --> 00:46:01.070
from inside yourself from stores, storage

00:46:01.070 --> 00:46:02.840
that you've established.

00:46:02.840 --> 00:46:04.410
So you can go outside of your space ship

00:46:04.410 --> 00:46:05.710
and you don't have to breathe the air

00:46:05.710 --> 00:46:08.900
'cause you have some source of air

00:46:08.900 --> 00:46:11.770
or whatever is your need
for staying alive inside you

00:46:11.770 --> 00:46:13.970
but that only lasts a
certain amount of time.

00:46:13.970 --> 00:46:18.859
Is that any different from
an egg laid by a chicken?

00:46:18.859 --> 00:46:22.640
The egg cannot survive in
our atmosphere (mumbles)

00:46:22.640 --> 00:46:25.920
and it stays in there
until all the egg resources

00:46:25.920 --> 00:46:27.953
turn into a chicken that can.

00:46:27.953 --> 00:46:32.810
An egg is like a spacesuit for an embryo.

00:46:32.810 --> 00:46:34.105
An eggshell.

00:46:34.105 --> 00:46:35.010
Are you with me on--

00:46:35.010 --> 00:46:35.843
- I'm with you on it.

00:46:35.843 --> 00:46:37.920
Let me just give one more
10 second quick answer

00:46:37.920 --> 00:46:39.490
to this question.

00:46:39.490 --> 00:46:43.000
An astrobiologist once challenged me

00:46:44.030 --> 00:46:48.700
to tell her why a star is not alive

00:46:48.700 --> 00:46:50.930
and if I was unable to do that,

00:46:50.930 --> 00:46:54.560
which I wasn't then maybe all the stars

00:46:54.560 --> 00:46:57.620
out in the Universe are
a form of life, not DNA,

00:46:57.620 --> 00:46:58.950
but just as alive as we are.

00:46:58.950 --> 00:47:00.890
- Yeah, the stars can
reproduce themselves.

00:47:00.890 --> 00:47:03.130
They are born, live
out their lives and die

00:47:03.130 --> 00:47:04.570
and they have a metabolism.

00:47:04.570 --> 00:47:07.130
It satisfies almost every
definition of life a biologist

00:47:07.130 --> 00:47:09.775
would hold for us. (ringing bell)

00:47:09.775 --> 00:47:10.860
- All right.

00:47:10.860 --> 00:47:12.756
- The stars are alive!
- Alive!

00:47:12.756 --> 00:47:13.810
(laughing)

00:47:13.810 --> 00:47:16.749
- But do they have agency, that's the

00:47:16.749 --> 00:47:19.207
And if there's a
consciousness. (ringing bell)

00:47:19.207 --> 00:47:21.490
- And when does a star become alive?

00:47:21.490 --> 00:47:22.570
- Next.

00:47:22.570 --> 00:47:24.272
I think only have time for one more, go!

00:47:24.272 --> 00:47:27.357
- All right, Christopher
Johnson on Instagram asks,

00:47:27.357 --> 00:47:30.730
"There are soon to be an
explosion of CubeSats around Earth

00:47:30.730 --> 00:47:31.597
- Oh no!

00:47:31.597 --> 00:47:33.390
- "How do you see our
near future shaping out

00:47:33.390 --> 00:47:35.530
with space becoming more
accessible commercially

00:47:35.530 --> 00:47:37.280
and to the public?"

00:47:37.280 --> 00:47:39.690
- This is not a lightning round answer

00:47:39.690 --> 00:47:41.260
but the bottom line is that

00:47:41.260 --> 00:47:43.580
it's very exciting to have
all these satellites up there

00:47:43.580 --> 00:47:45.040
but it's also very dangerous

00:47:45.040 --> 00:47:47.500
both for science and for
human beings over time.

00:47:47.500 --> 00:47:48.900
We have to be very wise

00:47:48.900 --> 00:47:50.760
about how we send those things up there.

00:47:50.760 --> 00:47:52.900
It has to be very carefully controlled

00:47:52.900 --> 00:47:54.210
otherwise we're gonna really regret

00:47:54.210 --> 00:47:55.273
having all those things up there.

00:47:55.273 --> 00:47:56.520
- Can you even keep
track of where they are?

00:47:56.520 --> 00:47:59.770
- You can but again it requires
a lot of advanced planning.

00:47:59.770 --> 00:48:01.167
You can't just have a company go,

00:48:01.167 --> 00:48:03.270
"Send it up 1000 satellites, woo hoo!"

00:48:03.270 --> 00:48:04.842
That's gonna cause us all problems.

00:48:04.842 --> 00:48:06.760
- I would say the reason

00:48:06.760 --> 00:48:08.110
why we haven't been visited by aliens

00:48:08.110 --> 00:48:09.999
'cause they see all this
crap orbiting Earth.

00:48:09.999 --> 00:48:11.609
They say, "I'm not
coming near that place".

00:48:11.609 --> 00:48:12.442
(ringing bell)

00:48:12.442 --> 00:48:15.600
That's my sound bite. (laughing)

00:48:15.600 --> 00:48:16.867
So, Charles thanks for coming on StarTalk.

00:48:16.867 --> 00:48:18.530
- It was my pleasure, I had a great time!

00:48:18.530 --> 00:48:21.177
Thank you man, I really
really like coming on.

00:48:21.177 --> 00:48:24.230
